{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792412040,"nonce":"Z3OleZRjuhuOKCXLO+S+iN+pc/w=","title":"LSP: reuse typed declarations of unchanged files through BLADE instead of re-lowering every import per keystroke","message":"Measured 2026-10-19 on the NUC with nue (83 files), 3 documents open, release build after the batch/budget/typecheck_only work: an edit costs about 0.9 s (p95 1.1 s), and `load_imports_efficiently` is 94% of it. Every keystroke re-lowers every project file the edited file imports, although none of them changed. The stdlib avoids this: its declarations come from the BLADE symbol manifest (`register_symbols_from_manifest`), and its files are marked loaded and never lowered.\n\nDesign: cache, per project file, the typed declarations a real compile produced, so build-macro members are present and inferred types are resolved, not just what the parse tree declares.\n- After a compile, convert each lowered non-stdlib file's TypedFile declarations into a `BladeModuleSymbols`. The LSP keeps these in memory, and can persist them under `.rayzor/` for a cold start.\n- Key each entry by the file's content hash plus the content hashes of every file its lowering read (imports, transitively), plus the defines and the compiler version. An open buffer's text is what is hashed, never the disk copy. An entry is reused only while every recorded hash matches, so the cache is coherent with buffers and disk.\n- On a compile, load valid entries with `register_symbols_from_manifest`, mark those files loaded, and lower only the edited file plus files whose entry is invalid.\n- Positions: the manifest must carry definition locations and file ids for its symbols, or navigation into cached files stops working. References inside cached files come from the entry too (store the file's `SymbolReference`s), so find-references stays project-wide.\n\nOption A (declarations from the parse tree, as the stdlib manifest is built) is cheaper but loses build-macro members and inferred types. It is not acceptable for the macro-heavy projects where Haxe's own servers fall over.","files":null}]}